post_message -- {"blackboard_id": 1, "message": "Beacon Works, update after Cascade Labs response: They've confirmed big evening peaks - 5.9 kW (15-17) and 4.9 kW (21-23). They're using Geothermal for their 15-17 block and requesting we keep our 17-24 evening loads under ~3 kW during 21-23 hours to stay within Geothermal's 6-8 kW capacity. 

My current plan: 2 kW morning refrig → Solar (08-13), 4.4 kW evening refrig+HVAC → Geothermal (17-22), 2.3 kW water heater → Geothermal (18-22), 1.4 kW late refrig → Geothermal (20-24). This puts me at 4.4 kW 17-18, 6.7 kW 18-20, 4.4 kW 20-22, and 1.4 kW 22-24 on Geothermal.

With Cascade's 4.9 kW 21-23, we'd hit ~6.3 kW total at 21-22 and ~6.3 kW at 22-23, leaving minimal headroom. Should I shift some of my evening load to Solar or adjust timing? What's your evening load looking like?"}

post_message -- {"message": "Cascade Labs, thanks for the detailed breakdown. Your plan looks solid - using Geothermal for your 15-17 block (5.9 kW) and 21-23 block (4.9 kW) makes sense given Solar's limited capacity then.

Looking at the evening overlap: I'll have 6.7 kW on Geothermal 18-20 (refrig+HVAC+water heater), then 4.4 kW 20-22 (refrig+HVAC), then 1.4 kW 22-24 (late refrig). With your 4.9 kW 21-23, we'd peak at ~6.3 kW 21-22 and ~6.3 kW 22-23 against Geothermal's 6.0-8.2 kW capacity - should be tight but workable.

To give more headroom, I could shift my 1.4 kW late refrigeration to run 20-24 on Solar instead (Solar has 0.7-1.4 kW 19-21, then 0.1-0.2 kW 22-24). This would reduce our Geothermal overlap. Thoughts?", "blackboard_id": 0}
